<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Kazys Škirpa</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1895">1895</a>–<a href="/years/1979">1979</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Kazys Škirpa was a Lithuanian army officer and diplomat, envoy in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/berlin">Berlin</a>, who founded the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lithuanian-activist-front">Lithuanian Activist Front</a> there in 1940 after the Soviet occupation of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lithuania">Lithuania</a>. He was named prime minister of the provisional government proclaimed during the June Uprising of 1941, but the Germans kept him under house arrest in Berlin. He later emigrated to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/kazys-skirpa.jpg" alt="Kazys Škirpa" width="320" height="432" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Kazys_Skirpa.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Kazys Škirpa was born on 18 February 1895 at Namajūnai. Mobilised into the Imperial Russian Army in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-world-war">World War I</a>, he graduated from the Peterhof Military School and in 1917 helped organise Lithuanian military units in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russia">Russia</a>, trying to form Lithuanian detachments in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/petrograd">Petrograd</a>. After <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lithuania">Lithuania</a> declared independence in 1918 he came home and was the first to enlist in its armed forces in the Lithuanian War of Independence. As commandant of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vilnius">Vilnius</a> he had his men raise the Lithuanian flag over <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gediminas">Gediminas</a>' Tower on 1 January 1919, the day now kept as Lithuania's flag day.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>Elected to the Constituent Assembly in 1920 for the Lithuanian Popular Peasants' Union, he went on to study at the Institute of Technology in Zurich, in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kaunas">Kaunas</a> and at the Royal Military Academy in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/belgium">Belgium</a>, and became Chief of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/german-general-staff">General Staff</a> in 1925. He was forced to resign after the 1926 coup d'état, having tried to gather troops to protect the government. He then represented <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lithuania">Lithuania</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a> from 1927 to 1930, at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/league-of-nations">League of Nations</a> in 1937, in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poland">Poland</a> in 1938 and in Germany again from 1938 to 1941.</p><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lithuanian-activist-front">Lithuanian Activist Front</a> he founded after the Soviet occupation aimed to restore independence by working with the Nazis. Kept from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lithuania">Lithuania</a> after the June rising of 1941, he was allowed only a short visit to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kaunas">Kaunas</a> in October 1943. In June 1944 he was arrested for a memorandum asking that German authorities in Lithuania be replaced by a Lithuanian government, and he was held at Bad Godesberg and, from February 1945, at Jezeří Castle.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>After the war he taught Russian at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/trinity-college-dublin">Trinity College Dublin</a>, emigrated to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a> in 1949 and worked at the Library of Congress. His memoir of the 1941 movement, Uprising for the Restoration of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lithuania">Lithuania</a>'s Sovereignty, appeared in 1975, and he died on 18 August 1979. In June 1995 his remains were reburied in Petrašiūnai Cemetery in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kaunas">Kaunas</a> with a state ceremony.</p><p>Streets, a plaque and a memorial stone honouring him have been disputed because of his anti-Semitic writings. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/genocide">Genocide</a> and Resistance Research Centre of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lithuania">Lithuania</a> denied that he had a role in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/holocaust">Holocaust</a> but acknowledged the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/antisemitism">anti-Semitism</a> of his writings, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kaunas">Kaunas</a> plaque remained. In July 2019 the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vilnius">Vilnius</a> city council renamed the alley named after him Trispalvė, Tricolour, while the Kaunas street kept his name.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Kazys Škirpa” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Its intended head, <b>Kazys Škirpa</b>, was held in Berlin, so Juozas Ambrazevičius served as acting prime minister.</blockquote><a href="/state/provisional-government-of-lithuania-1941">Provisional Government of Lithuania (1941)</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Pasvalys District Municipality, in what is now <a href="/countries/lithuania">Lithuania</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/washington-dc">Washington, D.C.</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/united-states">the United States</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=56.05,24.38&amp;year=1949" aria-label="Pasvalys District Municipality on the atlas"><svg class="map map-globe map-loc" viewBox="0 0 10000 10000" role="img" aria-label="Globe: the birthplace of Kazys Škirpa"><image href="/maps/globes/g030n60.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="10000"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M4732 5326h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M4732 5326h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=56.05,24.38&amp;year=1949">What was here in 1949? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
